    "q-162778": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162778",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
