    "q-37781": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37781",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-1\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(-2)^{2}=9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 3),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(0,-3)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,3),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$",
