      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$",
