    "q-98103": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98103",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 18.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(2,2).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=18.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=18\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-4=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(3,3),\\,(-3,-3),\\,(2,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(3,3)$ | $(-3,-3)$ | $(2,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $42$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-3,-3),$ and its maximum value is $42.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
