      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1$ is $(0,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 1).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 1),\\,(\\pm 1,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$",
