    "q-163055": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163055",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-6$ given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}\\le 5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-x=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$",
